# Motion & Scroll

`motion` v13 (import from `motion/react`) with `lenis` driving smooth scroll from
Motion's frame loop. Lenis moves the real native scroll position, so `useScroll`,
`position: sticky` and IntersectionObserver all work with no bridging.

## Never re-render on scroll

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — a React render every frame; this is the INP killer
const [y, setY] = useState(0);
useLenis(({ scroll }) => setY(scroll));

// ✅ GOOD — MotionValue updates the DOM outside React
const { scrollYProgress } = useScroll({ target: ref, offset: ["start end", "end start"] });
const y = useTransform(scrollYProgress, [0, 1], ["-8%", "8%"]);
return <motion.div style={{ y }} />;
```

State in a scroll callback is allowed only for values that change a handful of
times — an active index, or a threshold crossing via `useMotionValueEvent`.

## Rules

- `useScroll`'s `offset` defaults to `["start start", "end end"]`. Pass it explicitly.
- Don't wrap a scroll value in `useSpring`. Lenis is already lerping; double-smoothing
  feels worse and drops off the compositor-accelerated path.
- Prefer `whileInView` over `useInView` + state — it rides a pooled observer.
  Always pass `viewport={{ once: true }}` unless re-triggering is the point.
- `will-change` only on things that animate repeatedly (marquees, parallax layers).
  On every reveal element it makes the page slower, not faster.
- Reduced motion: `<MotionConfig reducedMotion="user">` covers Motion, and Lenis
  handles it natively. Only hand-roll for parallax driven by MotionValues.

## Client boundaries

Scroll hooks require `"use client"`. Keep those components as leaves — the page and
layout stay Server Components. A section that only needs a reveal should compose the
client `Reveal` primitive rather than becoming a client component itself.
